Permanode type:

camliNodeType: "importer"
importerType: "twitter"
authClientID:     "xxx"    // e.g. api token
authClientSecret: "sdkojfsldfjlsdkf"

camliNodeType: "importerAccount"
importerType: "twitter"
twitterAccount: "bradfitz"

Local mutable cache
-------------------

RunContext and SetupContext provide two automatically scoped sorted.KeyValue
views for local operational state:

- ImporterCache is shared by all accounts of one registered importer.
- AccountCache is private to one importer account, identified by its permanode.

Both views can use simple keys such as "ratelimit" without colliding with
other importers, accounts, or the other scope. Get, writes, batches, and Find
all stay within the selected scope, including unbounded scans. Close on a
cache view is a no-op, so an importer cannot close the shared backing store.
Cache updates do not create permanode claims and are not replicated with
imported blobs. Entries may be missing or stale after cache deletion. Deleting
the cache resets persisted state, not state already loaded by a running
importer; stop the server before removing it.

The server opens a file-backed sorted.DefaultKVFileType cache automatically
under osutil.CacheDir()/importer/. Each store is named with a SHA-256 hash of
the server base URL and importer handler prefix, keeping hosts isolated.
CacheDir respects PERKEEP_CACHE_DIR (or the legacy CAMLI_CACHE_DIR).

The low-level importer handlerArgs can optionally override the sorted backend:

    "handler": "importer",
    "handlerArgs": {
        "cache": {
            "type": "leveldb",
            "file": "/var/cache/perkeep/importer-cache.leveldb"
        }
    }

An explicit cache configuration must include its backend's required fields.
Separate hosts should use separate stores unless sharing is intentional.

For embedding and tests, NewHost defaults to a private in-memory cache.
HostConfig.Cache can supply another sorted.KeyValue; its caller owns its
lifetime, and Host.Close will not close it. Server-configured caches and the
default in-memory cache are owned by the Host. Host.Close cancels managed
importer runs and long polls, waiting up to one second for them to return and
owned storage to close. On timeout it returns an error; background cleanup
leaves the cache open until managed work returns, then closes it. Repeated
Close calls return the original result, even if cleanup later finishes. Stop
direct users of the Host, including independently invoked Importer.Run calls,
before closing it.
